A||N||4||worlb||remove pin stripe||||5||
Z||000000||worlb||12-17-2001||09:10 AM||||I recenty purchased a fiero fender from a forum member. It has pin striping tape that is fairly secure to fender. What solvent or process can I use on the fiero plastic to remove stripping and maintain paint and plastic integerty? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||GTDude||12-17-2001||09:20 AM||||Use a credit card and a hair dryer.  Heat the stipe area just hot enough for the adhesive to loose it's bond and use the credit card to gently slide between it and the fender.  I've had great luck with this method.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||watts||12-17-2001||10:23 AM||||That, or use this little rubber wheel from 3M that's designed just for doing that.<P>It's about the consistency of an eraser, and you run it in a drill, or other low speed device.  <P>Can't remember the exact name, but any good body supply store will sell it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||12-17-2001||10:27 AM||||make sure it hasnt been cleared over first or youll have a real mess. Just use a hair drier, and your fingnail and pull slowly. you can use alcohol to rub off any left over glue. Also, if its been on there very long, youll still see the stripes, because the paint under it didnt fade.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JD86GT350||12-17-2001||07:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by watts:<BR><B>That, or use this little rubber wheel from 3M that's designed just for doing that.<P>It's about the consistency of an eraser, and you run it in a drill, or other low speed device.  <P>Can't remember the exact name, but any good body supply store will sell it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>3M part number 7498 or 7499, depending upon if you need the mandrel or not<P>Astro also makes a cheaper (price and quality) one pn 400E||||reg||1
